      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "In registration trials, zafirlukast, an asthma medication, caused asymptomatic elevated aminotransferase levels in up to 5% of participants. Until now, however, no cases of severe hepatitis attributed to zafirlukast have been reported."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To report the clinical characteristics of three patients with severe hepatitis due to zafirlukast."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Case report."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "One community hospital and two university hospitals."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "Three middle-aged women taking zafirlukast, 20 mg twice per day."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTION",
          "content": "Discontinuation of zafirlukast therapy in three patients, steroid therapy in two patients, and orthotopic liver transplantation in one patient."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MEASUREMENTS",
          "content": "Serum aminotransferase and bilirubin levels, standard blood tests for causes of hepatitis other than drug toxicity, and liver biopsy in two patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Patient 1 recovered spontaneously, had a severe relapse after inadvertent rechallenge with the medication, and ultimately made a complete recovery. Patient 2 developed subfulminant hepatic failure and required liver transplantation. Patient 3 developed severe hepatitis that improved after treatment with corticosteroids. Liver tissue was available from two patients and showed histologic changes commonly associated with drug reactions."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Patients receiving zafirlukast may develop severe liver injury and should be observed for signs and symptoms of hepatitis."
        }
      ]},
